## 3 — Chrome Kiosk Launcher (`kiosk-launcher.txt`)

A text file at the **project root** that serves as a template for the `.bat` file used to launch Chrome in kiosk mode on Windows. The file must follow this structure:

### 3.1 — Internet Check

A ping loop that waits for connectivity before launching (configurable timeout, ~5 min default).

### 3.2 — Kill Stale Chrome

`taskkill /F /IM chrome.exe` to ensure a clean start.

### 3.3 — Per-Screen Chrome Instances

Each display gets its own `start` command with:

| Flag                                | Purpose                                                 |
| ----------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| `--kiosk`                           | True kiosk mode (primary screen)                        |
| `--start-fullscreen`                | Fullscreen mode (secondary screens)                     |
| `--user-data-dir`                   | Unique temp path per screen (allows parallel instances) |
| `--window-position`                 | Multi-monitor placement                                 |
| `--no-first-run`                    | Skip Chrome setup wizard                                |
| `--disable-infobars`                | Hide "Chrome is being controlled" bar                   |
| `--overscroll-history-navigation=0` | Disable swipe-to-go-back                                |
| `--disable-pinch`                   | Disable pinch zoom at browser level                     |
| `--disable-features=TranslateUI`    | Hide translation popups                                 |

### 3.4 — Delay Between Launches

Short `timeout` between screen launches so Windows can create each process cleanly.

> The URL, screen count, and monitor positions are project-specific — configure per deployment. Keep `kiosk-launcher.txt` in sync with the actual deployment `.bat`.
